  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 345 PM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Cold air damming and breezy/cool weather will \npersist into tonight. Dry weather and increasing sunshine will \nfollow for Fri through the middle of next week, with temps returning \nto above normal by early next week.  \n\nThe cold air damming regime remains in place today, with \nconsiderable low clouds and a stout, cool surface flow from the NE \nand N feeding lower dewpoints into the region, although partial \nclearing is taking place in the far northwest. An upper-level wave \nacross the southern Appalachians has triggered some patchy bands of \nlight rain and drizzle into the afternoon, mainly across the \nPiedmont and Sandhills. This light rain has started to dissipate in \nthe last few hours as the mid-level energy starts to skirt into SC \nand eventually pushes offshore tonight. As dry air continues to work \nin this evening, we should see a gradual erosion of the low stratus, \nmainly from west to east, slowest to erode over the Coastal Plain. \nGenerally lows in the upper 40s to mid 50s are expected by Friday \nmorning.\n\nThe slow-moving Ohio Valley mid level low will push into the eastern \nCarolinas late tonight through early Fri, as much lower PW spreads \ninto central NC from the N and NNW. The more deeply drier air and \nloss of dynamic forcing for ascent will lead to increasing sunshine \noverall, although clouds may linger for a longer time across our \nnortheast Fri, courtesy of the developing intense low just off the \nMid Atlantic coast. As this low wobbles and drifts slowly northward \noff the Northeast coast through the weekend and into early next \nweek, the center of the weakening parent high will retrograde WSW \ninto the central Great Lakes but continue to extend into our area. \nThis will equate to generally fair skies, dry weather, and a warming \ntrend, from below normal temps Fri to near normal readings by Sat \nand above normal readings for Sun through Thu. Highs should be back \ninto the 80s Tue/Wed, although afternoon dewpoints in the 50s to \naround 60s should keep conditions from becoming too uncomfortable.",
